# Rust build output
/target

# Hand-sculpted terrain (assets/world/edits.bin) is NOT ignored on purpose —
# it's authored content, not build output, and syncing it between machines is
# the whole reason this repo exists. It compresses well despite its size, being
# mostly zeros.

# OS cruft
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# Scripted figures build their own .blend from dev/art/*.py, so the .blend is
# an intermediate. Once one is taken over by hand in Blender, commit it and
# delete the script that made it — a generator and a hand-edited file cannot
# both be the source of truth.
/dev/art/*.blend
# And Blender's own rolling backup of one, which is the same intermediate a save
# older. This rule was missing, so a handful of *.blend1 are already tracked
# (cover_blade, cover_flower, cover_petals) — untracking those is a separate tidy.
/dev/art/*.blend1
/cover_patch.ply

# Scratch worktrees the workflow tool makes for parallel agents. They are full
# checkouts, so git sees them as embedded repositories and tries to add them.
/.claude/worktrees/

# Python bytecode. A stale ik_gait.pyc once made a source edit appear to do nothing.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The exporter sweeps every .blend in dev/art/, and `ranger.blend` is an authoring
# source whose product the game does not load - the warden is `person_ranger.glb`.
# So a local build regenerates `ranger.glb` and `git add -A assets/models` puts 18 MB
# of it straight back into the release tree. It did, once, undoing half of "Ship what
# the game loads, and nothing else".
/assets/models/ranger.glb

# Concept art and other authoring references live in dev/art/source/, NOT in assets/.
# The release workflows copy the whole asset tree, so anything left in there ships -
# two concept sheets at 4.3 MB were doing exactly that.
# EVERYTHING that is not a building the game loads. The rule was `*.png` and the
# next sheet to arrive was called `City Hall` with no extension at all, so it walked
# straight back into the shipped tree - the same 2.2 MB regression, one file later.
# A pattern that lists what to EXCLUDE has to guess what people will name things.
/assets/buildings/*
!/assets/buildings/*.json

# Where a capture run leaves its evidence. Fifteen PNGs and a report were landing in
# the repository root, one `git add -A` away from being committed as source.
/dev/evidence/

# And where `--photo` and `--matrix` leave theirs. The shots already committed here
# are the evidence Codex's reviews were written about and are named in them, so they
# stay - a rule cannot untrack what git is already tracking, which is exactly why
# this is the right rule. What it stops is the next visual pass adding another ten
# megabytes to a repository that is carrying 182 MB of pictures already.
#
# A capture worth keeping is one a document refers to: add it deliberately with
# `git add -f`, at a size worth the weight.
/dev/art/shots/
